<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Penataran</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Panataran</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Penataran is the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple complex of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, near Blitar on the slopes of Mount Kelud in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Built over some 250 years from the late 12th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, it served as a state temple dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and Majapahit kings, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>, visited it. Its terraces carry reliefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> stories.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/penataran.jpg" alt="Penataran" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pandu Aji Wirawan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangunan_Utama_Candi_Penataran_di_Blitar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The complex lies in the village of Penataran in Blitar Regency, roughly 12 km northeast of Blitar, on the lower southwestern slopes of the Kelud volcano. Construction began in 1197, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> era, and continued into the 15th century. The temple is mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarakretagama">Nagarakretagama</a>, which identifies it as the Palah temple and records that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a> visited it during his royal tour across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>; he is said to have considered it his favourite sanctuary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The complex is laid out in three courtyards, one behind the other, an arrangement that resembles the Balinese puras, with the most sacred structures in the rear courtyard nearest the mountain. The first courtyard holds two rectangular platforms that once carried thatched roofs on wooden pillars and gave space for rituals and ceremonies; the smaller is richly decorated with nagas, mythical snakes. Beside the entrance to the second courtyard stands a small stone shrine known as the Dated Temple, after a lintel inscribed with a date equivalent to 1323 found nearby. The second courtyard contains the Naga Temple, devoted to the nagas.</p><p>The main temple in the rear courtyard is dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. Its reliefs form one of the largest Indonesian collections showing the stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>'s avatars: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> story of the Javanese Ramayana and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> story as told in Triguna's Krishnayana. No stones that could have formed a stone roof have been found, so some scholars believe the main temple carried a tiered roof of wood and palm fibre or thatch, like the Pura Yeh Gangga near Perean on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, dated 1334. According to the Indonesian scholar R. Soekmono the central shrine held no divine image, marking a transition from the classical stone-roofed temple to the type now common in Bali.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Only the stone remains survive; the wooden and other perishable buildings and objects are lost, though reliefs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple near Prambanan show that such structures existed. The site was added to the UNESCO World Heritage Tentative List on 19 October 1995 in the cultural category, but in 2015 it was withdrawn from the list together with 11 other sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Penataran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court's patronage of Hindu-Buddhist traditions produced artistic and literary achievements, including the temples of <b>Panataran</b> and the brick architecture of Trowulan, and Old Javanese works such as the Nagarakretagama of Mpu Prapanca (1365), which describes the realm at its height.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
